ingest spoken word audio files

Usage:
    convert-audio [options] <inputDir> <outputDir>
Video files are ingested as well: their audio stream is extracted and converted
like any other input.
A transcript beside a source (<name>.srt, .vtt or .lrc, or with a language code:
<name>.en.srt) is written into its output: as timed lyrics in an Opus file, as a
subtitle track in an .m4a.
Options:
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -m, --mono      Force mono output.
    -s, --surround  Keep a surround source's channels, up to 7.1, instead of
                    downmixing it to stereo.
    -a, --adaptive  Adaptive mode: decide channels and bitrate per file rather
                    than globally. The output keeps the source's own channel
                    count - capped at stereo unless -s is given - and its target
                    bitrate is the spoken-word figure for that channel count
                    (55 kbps mono, 65 stereo, rising to 200 for 7.1), so a mono
                    source encodes mono at the lower rate, a stereo one stays
                    stereo, and with -s a multi-channel one gets its own rate.
                    Cannot be combined with -m or -b, and disables long-file
                    splitting.
    -c, --copy-others
                    Copy non transcoded files from <inputDir> into <outputDir>
    -e, --encoding <codec>
                    Output encoding: opus or aac or xheaac.
                    aac and xheaac are written as .m4a; xheaac needs an
                    external encoder (exhale).
                    Default opus
    -b, --bitrate <bitrate>
                    Bitrate of the output files
                    Default 46 kbps Opus or 64 kbps AAC, or 32 or 48 kbps
                    for forced mono output, and with -s the Opus default
                    scaled up with the channel count for a surround source
    -T, --threshold <kbps>
                    The bitrate a file must reach to be re-encoded.
                    Default 90 kbps, or 50 kbps for a mono run
    -j, --jobs <jobs>
                    Run up to <jobs> encoder processes in parallel.
                    Default one per CPU thread
    -g, --group <depth>
                    Judge the files under each folder <depth> levels into
                    <inputDir> together rather than one by one: all of them
                    are converted when their average bitrate reaches the
                    threshold, and none of them otherwise, so no folder comes
                    out half converted. A video, or a format that is always
                    converted, has its whole folder converted with it.
                    Cannot be combined with -a.
    -C, --ai-chapters
                    Add chapters to every output whose source has none, and no
                    <name>.chapters.txt beside it, worked out by a language
                    model (docs/models.md) from a transcript of the
                    speech, and write the transcript into the output as
                    well. A transcript sidecar is used rather than
                    transcribing again.
    -P, --prompt <text>
                    With -C: more instructions for the model, added to every
                    request, e.g. "a chapter per speaker, titled with their
                    name and topic". The model then reasons at medium effort,
                    and Claude may search the web.
    -O, --only <folder>
                    Only the files under <inputDir>/<folder>, converted to
                    where a run over all of <inputDir> would put them. May
                    be given more than once.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
    -x, --set <name=value>
                    Override a setting for this run, over the settings file;
                    repeatable. A secret is refused - the settings file is where
                    it goes. The settings page lists the names.
